I verified end-to-end reachability from the reported client (host_1_1_1_1) to every server in the 10.200.0.0/24 farm and DNS. ICMP from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6 and 10.200.0.2 succeeds with 0% loss and low RTTs (≈1–5 ms). HTTP (curl) from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.3 also returns immediately (≈2–5 ms total). On router_core_3, OSPF adjacencies are Full (two neighbors on eth0/eth1 as BDR and switch_server_access on eth2 as DR), and its routing table has the 10.200.0.0/24 prefix via 172.16.0.11 on eth2. The other cores (router_core_1 and router_core_2) also learn 10.200.0.0/24 via OSPF and have consistent paths. Host_1_1_1_1 has a valid default route to 10.1.1.1 and is exchanging traffic normally.

Affected component: none observed; OSPF control plane and data plane are healthy. Mechanism: Since OSPF neighbors are Full and the 10.200.0.0/24 route is present on all cores, traffic to the server farm forwards correctly through router_core_3 to switch_server_access. Localization: router_core_3 eth2 toward switch_server_access and all inter-core links are up and routing. Root cause: Not reproducible—no misconfiguration or failure on router_core_3 or the server farm; the outage reported likely occurred only during last night’s maintenance window and has since cleared. Corrective fix: No action required now. Recommend leaving as-is and enabling/confirming monitoring/alerts on OSPF adjacencies and server reachability to capture future transient events with timestamps. === END ===